VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-027
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe abdominal cramping and frequent loose stools requiring exclusion of severe dehydration or acute infectious colitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate sudden, frequent episodes of diarrhea in an older adult. Immediate clinical assessment was strictly required to rule out acute hypotensive dehydration, Clostridium difficile infection, or an acute surgical abdomen prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I've had to run to the toilet ten times since dinner, it's completely liquid, and I feel so weak and dizzy I can barely stand up.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports episodic loose stools in middle-aged/elderly females, historically diagnosed via biopsy. Tonight, the patient experienced an acute flare of severe, non-bloody watery diarrhea commencing 5 hours ago. Symptoms include intense pre-defecation lower abdominal cramping. The patient reports acute fatigue and subjective dizziness on standing. Denies vomiting, fevers, rigors, or recent travel/antibiotic use.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently fatigued and resting on the sofa.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, risk of progressive dehydration overnight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/75 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Alert, appearing mildly fatigued but conversing normally. No sunken eyes.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals hyperactive bowel sounds. Abdomen is soft, with mild, diffuse lower abdominal tenderness to deep palpation. No guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Cardiovascular exam shows no significant orthostatic drop in blood pressure (sitting 118/75, standing 115/78) and a capillary refill of 2 seconds. Mucous membranes are moist. History indicates a sub-epithelial collagen band on biopsy, symptoms easily managed with lifestyle/bismuth.
Environmental Observations: Clear path to the bathroom, safe living conditions.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent use of over-the-counter loperamide with minimal effect today.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Microscopic Colitis (Mild Collagenous) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Severe Hypovolemic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintained standing blood pressure, normal capillary refill, and moist oral mucosa.

Acute Clostridium difficile Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, no recent antibiotic exposure, and no severe systemic toxicity.

Ischemic Colitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of bloody stools and lack of severe, intractable abdominal pain out of proportion to exam findings.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, high-frequency watery diarrhea and subjective dizziness, raising immediate concern for acute hypovolemic dehydration in an older adult. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation to assess hemodynamic stability and determine the need for intravenous fluid resuscitation. Clinical examination confirmed the patient was euvolemic without orthostasis or signs of an acute surgical abdomen, attributing the flare to known microscopic colitis. This bedside assessment provided crucial reassurance, safely prevented an unnecessary late-night hospital admission for IV fluids, and allowed for immediate oral rehydration therapy management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an oral rehydration solution (ORS) and a dose of bismuth subsalicylate to rapidly decrease bowel mucosal secretion.
Positive Behavioral Change: Sip exactly 100mL of the oral rehydration solution every 15 minutes instead of gulping large amounts of plain water. By pacing the fluid intake with electrolytes, your bowel will absorb the liquid rather than flushing it straight out, which will immediately reduce the urgency to use the toilet.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 3-5 days to discuss maintenance therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department if you begin passing bright red blood or black, tarry stools, if you develop a high fever, or if you stop passing urine entirely for more than 8 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the trigger of the current flare; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The bowel lining is temporarily inflamed, causing it to leak fluid rather than absorb it. It will self-limit over the next 24 to 48 hours with proper symptomatic management.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you become so dizzy that you fall, or if you become confused or disoriented.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your colon is irritated and is currently unable to absorb water properly. The bismuth medication directly coats and calms the lining, while the sip-rehydration strategy replaces what you lost without triggering more cramps.
Actionable Steps: Stick strictly to the timed sipping of the rehydration salts. Avoid all dairy, caffeine, and artificial sweeteners tomorrow, as these will severely aggravate the colon.

